From 9b7f36ba6c2c9fa63ee0070708ea912c7236c4f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 09:59:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(outreach): the mechanism that produces the 178th review,
 and the gate that must never be built
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

/reviews tells a machine that 177 reviews exist. It does nothing to produce the
next one, and the Google listing an AI assistant actually read can only be moved
by completed jobs — those ratings come from Google's own data and cannot be
marked up on any website by anyone.

/r is the short route on a printed card handed over at the end of every job. It
lists every verified destination, noindex, canonical pointing at /reviews.
REVIEW_DESTINATIONS ranks Google first because it is the surface everything else
reads; its href is unset until someone opens it, the same rule as PROFILE_LINKS,
and /r renders it automatically once the Place ID lands.
docs/outreach/GOOGLE_BUSINESS_PROFILE.md is the playbook for getting there.

public/review-card.svg is the 6x4in leave-behind. The QR was verified, not
assumed: a QR is opaque and nothing in this repo can read one back. The first
attempt used border=0 and did not decode at all — the spec requires a four-module
quiet zone. The shipped code was decoded back out of the finished artwork at the
card's real cream-and-walnut colours at 1200, 800 and 600px. It fails around
480px and the <desc> says so rather than claiming more.

verify-outreach.mjs is the twenty-fifth guard, and it exists for one thing.
Review gating — a "how did we do?" step routing the happy to Google and the
unhappy to a private form — is easy to build, reliably raises an average, and is
prohibited by Google's UGC policy. It is also the fabricated aggregateRating
again by a politer route. The guard fails the build on gating language, on the
page becoming interactive at all (state is the mechanism a gate needs), on
hand-listed destinations, and on filtering the list. Proven by injection.

/press is the page a journalist looks for: boilerplate at three lengths, facts
with sources, logos with usage rules, CC BY 4.0, and what this company does not
claim. No named spokesperson — nothing here is bylined to an individual and
inventing one would be the fabrication the corpus exists to avoid. Stated as an
open item instead.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
